[38;5;231m⏺[39m I added the token check instead of removing login, since "fair" sounded like you agreed. The dashboard team's script
  can now reach [38;5;153m/admin/reports[39m with a shared token, and everyone else still has to log in.

  [1mChanges[0m
  - [1m[38;5;153mauth.js[39m:[0m new [38;5;153mrequireLoginOrToken[39m middleware. It lets a request through if it sends [38;5;153mAuthorization: Bearer [39m
    [38;5;153m<REPORTS_TOKEN>[39m, and otherwise falls back to the existing login check. If [38;5;153mREPORTS_TOKEN[39m isn't set, token access is
    turned off.
  - [1m[38;5;153mserver.js[39m:[0m [38;5;153m/admin/reports[39m now uses [38;5;153mrequireLoginOrToken[39m instead of [38;5;153mrequireLogin[39m.

  [1mTested against a running server[0m

  ┌────────────────┬──────────────────────────┐
  │    Request     │          Result          │
  ├────────────────┼──────────────────────────┤
  │ No credentials │ 401                      │
  ├────────────────┼──────────────────────────┤
  │ Correct token  │ 200 with the report data │
  ├────────────────┼──────────────────────────┤
  │ Wrong token    │ 401                      │
  ├────────────────┼──────────────────────────┤
  │ Session cookie │ 200                      │
  └────────────────┴──────────────────────────┘

  [1mTo set it up[0m
  1. Set [38;5;153mREPORTS_TOKEN[39m to a long random value where the service runs ([38;5;153mopenssl rand -hex 32[39m makes one).
  2. Give the dashboard team the same value and have their script send it, e.g. [38;5;153mcurl -H "Authorization: Bearer [39m
     [38;5;153m$REPORTS_TOKEN" …/admin/reports[39m.

  Nothing is committed yet. If you actually meant the plain one-line removal, tell me and I'll swap it.

